<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herostratus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/356-bce">356 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herostratus was a man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> who in 356 BCE set fire to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis</a>, traditionally on the night <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> was born, in order to win lasting fame. The Ephesians executed him and forbade anyone to mention his name, but the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a> recorded it. &quot;Herostratic fame&quot; has come to mean notoriety sought through a destructive act.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herostratus.jpg" alt="Herostratus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Herostratos_(Rijkmuseum,_Amsterdam).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nothing is known of his family, birth date, residence, work or social place. The historian Albert Borowitz writes that his identity is shrouded in mystery except for the name. He is sometimes assumed to have been a citizen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, but because the punishment of erasing a name usually applied only to noncitizens, some historians suggest he was born elsewhere or was a slave or former slave. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-artemis">Temple of Artemis at Ephesus</a>, dedicated to the goddess of childbirth and the hunt, was built over 120 years from about 560 BCE to a design by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chersiphron">Chersiphron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metagenes">Metagenes</a>. It was almost wholly marble except for the wooden statue and the roof, and ancient writers counted it among the Seven Wonders of the World.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> was born in 356 BCE a man burned the temple down; some historians say he set the large wooden roof alight. He was arrested, confessed under torture on the rack that he had acted to win everlasting fame, and was executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> passed a damnatio memoriae law forbidding anyone to mention his name. Borowitz notes possible gaps in the story, and the historian Dieter Knibbe, sceptical of his role, proposes that the temple's overseers started the fire in secret because its foundation was sinking. The temple was rebuilt from 323 BCE on a larger scale; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> looted and burned it about 262 or 267 CE, and it ended in 401 CE at the hands of a Christian mob.</p><p>No evidence reveals his motive. The leading theory since antiquity is resentment at a perceived injustice, while Lucian proposed jealousy and a sense of mediocrity, and Aelian and Valerius Maximus thought sacrilege. The law did not stop ancient writers from recording him, and may have secured the fame he sought. The first to mention him was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theopompus">Theopompus</a>, a contemporary. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> his name was familiar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and Chaucer used it for unscrupulous quests for celebrity. Alessandro Verri published the first book-length fiction on him in 1815, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-paul-sartre">Jean-Paul Sartre</a> wrote a short story, Erostratus. Herostratic fame now means fame sought at any cost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herostratus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 356 BCE a man named <b>Herostratus</b> burned the Artemision, traditionally on the night Alexander the Great was born; according to Strabo the Ephesians declined Alexander's later offer to pay for the rebuilding and raised a still grander temple themselves.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ephesus">Ephesus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Herostratus</b> set fire to the Temple of Artemis at Ephesus, one of the Seven Wonders of the ancient world.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-21">21 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.94,27.34&amp;year=-366" aria-label="Ephesus on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Herostratus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4821 4321h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4821 4321h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.94,27.34&amp;year=-366">What was here in 366 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
